  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAfternoon satellite and water vapor loops show the \nmid/upper trough deepening and becoming negatively tilted across the \nGreat Lakes and Northeast CONUS. Current RAP analysis places the \nsurface low over western New York, with the trailing cold front \napproaching the U.S. 30 corridor. A band of scattered showers along \nand ahead of the front driven by weak instability and low-level \nconvergence is exiting a Mahoning to Knox County line. Expect most \nof the showers to be done by the time the front exits late this \nafternoon, but lingered some slight chance POPS through late \nafternoon due to diurnal heating and progressively cooler air aloft \nthat could trigger an isolated shower or two. Dew points remain \nelevated this afternoon, but a secondary surface trough currently \ncrossing the lake will start to bring in the true airmass change \nthis evening.\n\nFor tonight, most areas will stay dry as the drier airmass continues \nto advect in, but the surface low will become vertically stacked \nbeneath the closing off mid-level low over upstate New York. This \nwill lead to a deepening cyclonic flow cold air advection pattern \nwhich should produce scattered lake enhanced rain showers in far NE \nOhio and NW PA. Wraparound moisture supports the idea of some \nshowers, but RAP forecast soundings show lake to 850 mb Delta T's \nonly averaging about 10 C along with shallow moisture profiles \nconfined below 800 mb. An inversion will also be present around 800 \nmb, so this shallow moisture and low inversion environment will keep \nthe rain showers light and disorganized. With this all being said, \nlimited POPS to slight chance in far NE Ohio and NW PA tonight. \n\nBy Wednesday, the drier air should win out and allow any lake \nshowers to end as the closed low gradually drifts farther east and \nCanadian high pressure builds in from the Upper Midwest. This high \nwill settle over the Lower Great Lakes and Ohio Valley Thursday into \nFriday allowing the pleasant temperatures and low humidity to \ncontinue through the end of the week. Lows in the mid 50s away from \nthe lake both Thursday and Friday mornings will give the air \nconditioners a nice break. \n\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe Canadian high will shift into the Mid Atlantic \nregion this weekend allowing return southerly flow to develop as the \nold closed low over the NE U.S weakens and lifts out. At the same \ntime, another northern stream mid/upper shortwave trough will drop \nout of the Upper Midwest Saturday and evolve into a closed low over \nthe Ohio and Tennessee Valleys by Sunday. Deterministic and \nensemble guidance is struggling with the exact evolution and \nultimate track of this closed low and how long it will linger, \nwhich is common in these set ups. Confidence is highest in a \nreturn to warmer and more humid conditions given the enhanced \nwarm air advection between the high to the east and closed low \ndiving to the west, but coverage of convection in this airmass \nis much lower confidence. Kept NBM chance POPS Saturday through \nTuesday at this time, with the greatest coverage during daytime \nheating. Some areas that have missed out on rain the past couple\nof weeks may see some with the scattered convection between \nthis weekend and early next week as we turn the calendar to \nAugust.",
